b/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093859 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7bc385c7-474c-5cbc-b16e-9c8a45d2714e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095826 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095826 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095826 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095826 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095826 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095826 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095826 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095826 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "93fc0622-6b89-5e60-84fd-c344c741bb85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095827 #edb/ref 1080 "\\mid R_{2} \\mid \\le \\frac{3}{800}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095827 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095827 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "decbd5ef-e64b-5cfb-8888-0027cc73993e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095828 #edb/ref 1080 "\\mid R_{2} \\mid \\le \\frac{3}{11000}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095828 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095828 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f5018280-f2bd-5e10-82a9-5edd58c167cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095829 #edb/ref 1080 "\\mid R_{2} \\mid \\le \\frac{9}{400}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095829 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095829 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0cbb753a-a19e-521d-8436-94871efc85eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095830 #edb/ref 1080 "\\mid R_{2} \\mid \\le \\frac{9}{8000}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095830 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095830 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1ab177ad-8935-567c-a148-c29f4ea2662d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095831 #edb/ref 1080 "\\mid R_{2} \\mid \\le \\frac{27}{400}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095831 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095831 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6a28873b-6ca0-525d-9aff-2a156fd2ac14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096562 #edb/ref 1069 "e-745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096562 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)=e^{x}$ and let $P_2(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1$. Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when $P_2(x)$ is used to approximate $e^{1.1}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c946c1c9-f3d2-5d9d-9390-8ee542ca15a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096562 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error $R_2$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial $P_2$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}R_2 & \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}\\end{vmatrix}x-\\left.1\\right|^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $\\mid f^{(3)}(x) \\mid$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=1.1$. Plugging $x=1.1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\mid R_{2} \\mid \\mid \\le \\frac{M}{3!} \\mid 1.1 - \\left.1\\right|^{3};\\left. \\mid R_{2} \\mid \\mid \\le \\frac{M}{6} \\mid 0.1\\right|^{3}\n$$\n\nTo find $M$, we need to compute $\\mid f^{(3)}(x) \\mid$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,1.1]$. Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= e^{x} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= e^{x} \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= e^{x} \\\\\nf^{(3)}(x) &= e^{x} \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}f^{(3)}(x) & &= & e^{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= e^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid f^{(3)}(x) &= e^x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis an increasing function on the interval $[1,1.1]$, its value is greatest at $x=1.1$. So\n\n$$\nM = \\mid f^{(3)}(1.1) \\mid =e^{1.1}\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{vmatrix}R_2 & \\leq \\dfrac{e^{1.1}}{6}\\end{vmatrix}0.1\\right|^{3} \\approx 0.000\\,501\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096563 #edb/ref 1069 "e-746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096563 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)=\\ln(1-x)$ and let $P_3(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0$. Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when $P_3(x)$ is used to approximate $\\ln(0.9)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096563 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f5e5945-cdaa-55b9-b360-9e68249480b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096563 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error $R_3$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial $P_3$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}R_3 & \\leq \\dfrac{M}{4!}\\end{vmatrix}x-\\left.0\\right|^{4}\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $\\mid f^{(4)}(x) \\mid$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=0.1$. Plugging $x=0.1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\mid R_{3} \\mid \\mid \\le \\frac{M}{4!} \\mid 0.1 - \\left.0\\right|^{4};\\left. \\mid R_{3} \\mid \\mid \\le \\frac{M}{24} \\mid 0.1\\right|^{4}\n$$\n\nTo find $M$, we need to compute $\\mid f^{(4)}(x) \\mid$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,0.1]$. Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{1}{x - 1} \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= - (1)/((x - 1)^{2}) \\\\\nf^{‴}(x) &= (2)/((x - 1)^{3}) \\\\\nf^{(4)}(x) &= - (6)/((x - 1)^{4}) \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}f^{(4)}(x) & &= & - (6)/((x - 1)^{4})\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (6)/((x - 1)^{4})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid f^{(4)}(x) &= \\dfrac{6}{(x-1)^4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis an increasing function on the interval $[0,0.1]$, its value is greatest at $x=0.1$. So\n\n$$\nM = \\mid f^{(4)}(0.1) \\mid =\\dfrac{6}{(0.1-1)^4} = \\dfrac{6}{(0.9)^4}\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{vmatrix}R_3 & \\leq \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{6}{(0.9)^4} \\right)}{24}\\end{vmatrix}0.1\\right|^{4} \\approx 0.000\\,038\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186096971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "360b8076-1b27-5630-a07e-221a0e53011d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186096961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186096980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096821 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096821 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096821 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "79a10073-8798-597e-821c-691b0dad5ae7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1045 "The Lagrange Error Bound" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186097751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f055ed71-ae5b-569a-8cbe-acd4850a5cce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096980 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186093854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096980 #edb/ref 1069 "q-11585" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096980 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x) = e^{x}$ and let $P_{3}(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x = 1$. Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error $R_{3}$ obtained when $P_{3}(x)$ is used to approximate $e^{0.65}$. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096980 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f0c0766-f92b-5029-93d1-2aeb7633b5b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096980 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097317 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097317 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097317 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x) = \\ln x$ and let $P_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x = 2$. Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error $R_{2}$ obtained when $P_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\ln 2.3$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097317 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7bfbcc6e-0a0e-51f6-8574-b8c524edf820" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097317 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097751 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097751 #edb/ref 1061 4844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097751 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c93a74b3-e513-5fb9-9595-6bf177ec940a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097751 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097752 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097752 #edb/ref 1061 4845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097752 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "82542d7a-3ec1-5979-8dd2-13a732ad5eb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097752 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097753 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097753 #edb/ref 1061 4849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097753 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13415159-b315-5389-bc81-61ef044820e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097753 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097754 #edb/ref 1061 4850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097754 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b70fcb39-7efc-50ad-a28c-73509579c61d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097754 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097803 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to *approximate*\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x\n$$\n\nfor some value of $x$. We can do this using the second-degree Taylor polynomial about a point $x=a$. For example, the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $e^x$ about the point $x=1$ is shown below:\n\n$$\ne^x \\approx P_2(x) = e + e(x-1) + \\dfrac 1 2 e(x-1)^2\n$$\n\nIn general, the polynomial $P_2(x)$ is only an approximation to the function $f(x)$, which means that there will usually be some error whenever we use $P_2$ to approximate $f$. We want to know how bad this error can get. Can we find an upper bound for the error?\n\nLet $R_2(x)$ be the **error** (or **remainder**) that we get when we use $P_2$ to approximate\n\n$$\nf(x)=e^x\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\nR_2(x) = e^x - P_2(x)\n$$\n\nThe **Lagrange error bound** states that an upper bound for $R_2$ is given by,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}R_2 & \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}\\end{vmatrix}x-\\left.a\\right|^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ the maximum value of $\\mid f^{(3)}(x) \\mid$ on the interval between $a$ and $x$.\n\nIn our case, since our Taylor polynomial is taken about the point $a=1$, the Lagrange error bound becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}R_2 & \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}\\end{vmatrix}x-\\left.1\\right|^{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that the Lagrange error bound looks a lot like the last term of the *next* Taylor polynomial, $P_3(x)$.\n\nThe tricky part is finding the value of $M$. We'll learn how to do that in the following example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097803 #edb/ref 1065 1292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097803 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fcb4053a-c61a-538b-8b44-5a92f73f053f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097803 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097804 #edb/ref 1064 "The Lagrange error bound that we've discussed so far is valid only for the error associated with a second-degree Taylor polynomial. But we can generalize the result to higher-order Taylor polynomials.\n\nLet $R_n(x)$ be the error involved in approximating a function $f(x)$ by its $n$th-degree Taylor polynomial $P_n(x)$ about $x=a$. So, we have\n\n$$\nR_n(x) = f(x) - P_n(x)\n$$\n\nThen the Lagrange error bound for $R_n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}R_n(x) & \\leq \\dfrac{M}{(n+1)!}\\end{vmatrix}x-\\left.a\\right|^{n+1}\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $\\mid f^{(n+1)} \\mid$ on the interval between $a$ and $x$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097804 #edb/ref 1065 1294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097804 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "07b10e6a-1673-5d6e-8572-8b4505579f44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097804 #edb/ref 1067 "The General Result of the Lagrange Error Bound" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
